  "summary": "Pixels with depth: whole worlds built from cubes, lit like dioramas.",
  "description": "Voxel art treats the cube as the atomic brush: volumetric grids rendered in tools like MagicaVoxel, or driven in-engine from Ultima Underworld through Minecraft. The modern scene flourished after ephtriu's MagicaVoxel (2015) made 8-bit-shaded voxel dioramas one-click renderable — tiny cities, cyberpunk ramen stands, medieval keeps, all readable at a glance yet constructed cube by cube. Games (Resogun, Teardown, voxel roguelikes) use voxels for destructibility and charm; artists like Sir carma built followings on single-cube craftsmanship. The aesthetic is digital sculpture: isometric mass, hard-edge color steps, miniature-world storytelling.",
  "culturalContext": "Voxel art sits at the crossroads of game culture and digital sculpture: MagicaVoxel's free tool democratized it, Minecraft normalized cube-thinking for a generation, and voxel dioramas are now a staple of indie game marketing, NFT-era art discourse and 3D-printing communities.",
